{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "measuredBy": "lane DDL-LOCK-CENSUS",
  "measuredOn": "2026-09-22, dev clone jxhgzalwckuarngvsdyq, PostgreSQL 17.6",
  "method": "Each statement ran inside its own BEGIN \u2026 ROLLBACK on a COMMITTED scratch estate (schema ddlcensus: one plain table, one 4-partition hash parent), and the locks were read from the measuring backend's own pg_locks (pid = pg_backend_pid()), counting only relations that already existed before the probe \u2014 a brand-new index locking itself is not contention. CONCURRENTLY cannot run in a transaction: it was measured from a second connection while a third held the build waiting. Run twice; strongest lock, hook membership and ACCESS EXCLUSIVE count identical on both runs (own-relation counts wobble by 1-2 through our own event triggers touching platform.entity_types).",
  "hookSetSize": 23,
  "hook": "Supabase's supautils.policy_grants, context sighup, unchangeable by us. It fires on CREATE/ALTER/DROP POLICY and on DROP TRIGGER \u2014 and, measured here, on NOTHING ELSE: drop table, alter column type, truncate and drop index take ACCESS EXCLUSIVE without it. TRIGGER-LOCK's sentence 'it fires on DDL generally' is too broad.",
  "partitionedParents": {
    "custom.record": 16,
    "history.row_versions": 29
  },
  "rule": "A statement class is WINDOW-CLASS at --target production when its measured footprint either (a) includes the hook set, or (b) holds ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on a partitioned parent. Nothing here is decided by the statement NAME.",
